{
  "world_setting": "## Douglass Park Commons\nDouglass Park Commons is a restored greystone courtyard building on the edge of Douglass Park in North Lawndale, on Chicago's West Side, in the year 2045. Built in the 1910s and rebuilt by a neighborhood land trust, it houses teachers, nurses, coders, line cooks, tradespeople, musicians, students, retirees and newcomers from across the country and the world. Some grew up on these blocks; others came from New York, the South, Mexico, West Africa or Eastern Europe. They share the same courtyard, the same back porches and the same thin walls.\n\n## The City in 2045\nChicago still runs on the 'L', CTA buses and Metra, on Lake Shore Drive and the expressways, on the lakefront and its neighborhoods. The city has bet on right-sized local AI: routine public work runs on small neighborhood models instead of distant clouds. North Lawndale's West Side Cyber Commons trains teens and elders side by side to defend the city's systems.\n\n## Familiar Places\n1. The Courtyard\nGrills, folding chairs and kids' bikes in summer; snow-shovel duty and porch-light gossip in winter.\n2. The Corner Coffee Shop on Ogden\nMost residents stop in almost every day. It feels like the building's second living room.\n3. Douglass Park\nPick-up games, the field house, summer festivals and long walks.\n4. The Pink Line\nThe 'L' a few blocks away: twenty-some minutes to the Loop on a good day.\n\n## What the Building Means\nTo Chicago, this is one greystone among thousands. To the people who live here it is where strangers become friends, friends become family, and a neighborhood decides what its future looks like.",
